## Pictures as tokens

A transformer doesn't care what its tokens stand for. All it needs is a row of vectors (Ch 5). Text
becomes that row by splitting into tokens. A picture can do the same by being cut into square
**patches**, each turned into a vector by multiplying its pixel values by learned weights, plus a
position, just like a token. The rest is the familiar stack of blocks. This is the *vision
transformer*.<Cite id="dosovitskiy2021" />

Here it is with CLIP ViT-B/32, a real model, on simple pictures drawn for this page. It cuts each
224×224-pixel picture into a 7×7 grid of 32×32-pixel patches: 49 "tokens". Pick a patch:

<PatchCutter />

Notice that patches of plain background all turn into the same vector, the way the same word always
starts as the same embedding.

## A shared map for words and pictures

CLIP was trained on 400 million pictures paired with their captions from the internet. It has two
parts, one that reads pictures and one that reads text, and training pushed each picture's vector
close to its own caption's vector and away from the others.<Cite id="radford2021" /> The result is a
single space where words and pictures can be compared. Pick some words and see which pictures land
closest:

<PictureMatch />

For each of the 12 exact descriptions, the right picture comes first. Vaguer words are harder:
"something round" barely prefers a circle.

## Chat models that see

A <GlossaryPopover term="multimodal">multimodal</GlossaryPopover> chat model puts the two ideas
together. A vision model like CLIP's turns the picture into patch vectors, a small layer converts
those into the same kind of vectors as text tokens, and the language model reads them in its
context alongside your words.<Cite id="liu2023" /> To the language model, the picture is just more
tokens.

<RecapCard>

- A vision transformer cuts a picture into patches and treats each patch like a token.
- CLIP puts pictures and captions into one shared space, so words can find matching pictures.
- Chat models that see turn a picture into tokens and put them in the context next to the text.

</RecapCard>

**Next question:** these models read pictures. How do image generators make them?
